<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the entropy change (ΔS) for the given reaction, we use the formula:

\[ ΔS = Σ [S_{observed</sub>] - Σ [S_{initial}] \]

Where Σ represents the sum of the molar entropities of the products minus the sum of the molar entropities of the reactants.

For the given reaction:

\[ 2 \text{BiS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]

The molar entropities are:

- Bromene (BrS) = 91.2
- Oxygen(O_2) = 205.1
- Polane (PolO) = 66.5
- Sulfane (SolE) = 248.2

Now, let's plug these values into the formula:

\[ ΔS = (2 \times 66.5 + 2 \times 248.2) - (2 \times 91.2 + 3 \times 205.1) \]

Calculate the sum of the products:

\[ 2 \times 66.5 = 133.0 \]
\[ 2 \times 248.2 = 496.4 \]
\[ Total (products) = 133.0 + 496.4 = 629.4 \]

Calculate the sum of the reactants:

\[ 2 \times 91.2 = 182.4 \]
\[ 3 \times 205.1 = 615.3 \]
\[ Total (reactants) = 182.4 + 615.3 = 797.7 \]

Now, subtract the reactants from the products:

\[ ΔS = 629.4 - 797.7 = -168.3 \]

The entropy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-168.3 \text{ g/mol K}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

