Next, let's find the volume of 3.00 M H\(_2\)O\(_2\), which is 20.0 liters. Since the reaction is 1:1, the volume of the product is the same as the volume of the reactants.

Now, we can set up the equation to find the volume of 6.00 M H\(_2\)SO\(_4\), which we will call \(x\):

\[
\frac{10.0 \text{ L} \times 2.00 \text{ N}}{10.0 \text{ L}} + \frac{x \text{ L} \times 6.00 \text{ M}}{10.0 \text{ L}} = 20.0 \text{ L} \times 3.00 \text{ M}
