To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction between [Fe(H20)6]2+ and 2Cl1 atoms to produce [FeCl4]2+ and 2H20, we use the Beer-Lambert law:

ΔFe = 340 kJ/mol
ΔCl = 381 kJ/mol

The reaction can be represented as:

Fe(H20)62+ + 2Cl1 → [FeCl4]2+ + 2H20

The standard enthalpy change (ΔH) for this reaction can be calculated using the following formula:

ΔH = Σ(ΔH_obs_i) - Σ(ΔH_obs_i)

Where ΔH_obs_i is the observed enthalpy of the compound.

The observed enthalpy of the compound is the sum of the enthalpy of formation of the compound and the enthalpy of the reactants.

The enthalpy of formation of [FeCl4]2+ is given as -392 kJ/mol. The enthalpies of hydrogenation for Fe2+ and Cl1 are also given as -340 kJ/mol and -381 kJ/mol, respectively.

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction can be calculated as follows:

ΔH = (340 kJ/mol + 381 kJ/mol) - (-392 kJ/mol)
ΔH = 721 kJ/mol + 392 kJ/mol
ΔH = 1113 kJ/mol

Therefore, the standard enthalpy change for the reaction between [Fe(H20)6]2+ and 2Cl1 ions to produce [FeCl4]2+ compound ion is $\boxed{1113}$  kJ/mol.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

