Now, let's denote the time B takes to catch up to A as \( t_b \).

For A, the total time he travels is \( t_a = t_b + 3 \).

The distance A travels is \( d_a = v_a \times t_a = 10 \times (t_b + 3) \).

The distance B travels is \( d_b = v_b \times t_b = 20 \times t_b \).

When B catches up to A, they will have covered the same distance from the start. So, \( d_a = d_b \).

\[
10 \times (t_b + 3) = 20 \times t_b
\]

\[
10t_b + 30 = 20t_b
\]

\[
30 = 10t_b
\]

\[
t_b = 3 \text{ hours}
\]

Now, we can find the distance B travels to catch up to A:

\[
d_b = 20 \times t_b = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km}
\]

So, B catches up to A after 3 hours from B's start, and they are 60 km from the start.

