What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by the formula:

\[
\text{D} = \frac{lambda}{2\pi \left(n_{\text{eff}} - n_{\text{f}}\right)}
\]

where:
- \( D \) is the decay length
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the wave
- \( n_{\text{eff}} \) is the effective refractive number of the waveguide
- \( n_{\text{f}} \) is the refractive number of the surrounding field (e.g., the surrounding air or a waveguide)

Substituting the given values:
\[
\lambda = 1 \text{ m}
\]
\[
n_{\text{eff}} - n_{\text{f}} \approx 0.3
\]

Substituting these into the formula:

\[
D = \frac{1 \text{ m}}{2\pi \times 0.3} \approx \frac{1}{1.885} \text{ m} \approx 0.531 \text{ m}
\]

Thus, the decay length is approximately \(\boxed{0.531 \text{ meters}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

