\[
k = k_0 e^{-E_a/RT}
\]  
where \( k \) is the rate constant, \( k_0 \) is the rate constant at infinity (i.e., \( \infty \)), \( E_a \) is the activation energy, \( R \) is the gas constant, and \( T \) is the molar concentration.  

The Arrhenius equation can be linearized by taking the natural logarithm of both sides:  
\[
\ln C = \ln k_0 - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]  

Using two given points, we can find the slope and intercept of the linear plot of \( \ln C \) versus \( \frac{1}{T} \):  
\[
\frac{1}{T_1} = 0.0018 \, \text{K}^{-1}, \quad k_{f1} = 10^{-1} \, \text{J}^{-1} \text{s}^{-1}
\]  
\[
\frac{1}{T_2} = 0.0012 \, \text{K}^{-L}, \quad k_{f2} = 10^4 \, \text{J}^{-1} \text{s}^{-1}
\]  

The slope of the linear line is \( -\frac{E_a}{RT} \), and the intercept is \( \ln C_0 \).  

Using the two given points, we can find the slope \( m \):  
\[
m = \frac{\ln C_0 - \ln C_1}{\frac{1}{T_2} - \frac{1}{T_1}} = \frac{\ln \frac{k_2}{k_1}}{\frac{1}{T_2} - \frac{1}{T_1}}
\]  

Substituting the given values:  
