","The covariant derivative of a vector \( V^\mu \) in curved spacetime is a more-general version of the ordinary derivative, which accounts for the change of the basis basis components \( e^{i\mu} \) (the components of the metric metric metric) when moving through spacetime. 

For a vector \( V^\mu \), the covariant derivative is denoted as \( \nabla V^\mu \) and is given by:

\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^i} + \frac{1}{2} g^{mn} g^{ij} g^{jk} g^{kl} \frac{\partial e^i}{\partial x^n} V^m \frac{\partial e^j}{\partial x^k} V^l
\]

This can be simplified using the Christoffel symbols \( \Gamma^{i}_{kl} \):

\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^i} + \Gamma^{i}_{kl} V^k \frac{\partial x^l}{\partial x^i}
\]

This is the most common form of the covariant derivative for a vector. 

For example, if \( V^\mu \) is a vector, the covariant derivative of \( V^\mu \) is:

\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^i} + \Gamma^{i}_{kl} V^k \frac{\partial x^l}{\partial x^i}
\]

This is a vector with the same components as \( V^\mu \). 

For a tensor \( T^{i_1 i_2 \ldots i_n} \), the covariant derivative is:

\[
\nabla T^{i_1 i_2 \ldots i_n} = \frac{\partial T^{i_1 i_2 \ldots i_n}}{\partial x^i} + \Gamma^{i_1}_{kl} T^{kl i_2 \ldots i_n} + \Gamma^{i_2}_{kl} T^{i_1 kl i_3 \ldots i_n} + \cdots + \Gamma^{i_n}_{kl} T^{i_1 i_2 \ldots kl}
\]

This is a tensor with the same indices as \( T^{i_1 i_2 \ldots i_n} \). 

This is a more-general version of the ordinary derivative, which accounts for the change of the basis basis components \( e^{i\mu} \) (the components of the metric metric metric) when moving through spacetime. 

