An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The forward voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback fraction \( m_f = 0.1 \). The voltage gain decreases by 6 dB, which means the new forward voltage gain \( A_v' = A_v \times \frac{1}{2.5} = 100 \times \0.4 = 40 \).

The forward and feedback transfer ratios are \( A_v \) and \( m_f \), respectively. The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:

\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_f A_v}
\]

For the original \( A_v = 100 \) and \( m_f = 0.1 \):

\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1 \times 100} = \frac{100}{1 - 10} = \frac{100}{-9} = -11.11 \text{ (negative feedback)}
\]

For the modified \( A_v' = 40 \) and \( m_f = 0.1 \):

\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{40}{1 - 0.1 \times 40} = \frac{40}{1 - 4} = \frac{40}{-3} = -13.33 \text{ (negative feedback)}
\]

The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:

\[
\text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{-13.33 - (-11.11)}{-11.11} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{-2.24}{-11.11} \right) \times 100\% = 0.201 \times 100\% = 20.1\%
\]

Thus, the percent change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{20.1\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

