<USER_REQUEST>
A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The time dilation principle states that the time interval \( t_s \) on a spaceship (stationary in respect to the spaceship) is half the time interval \( t_e \) on Earth:
\[
t_e = \sqrt{2} t_s
\]
Given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, we can use this time as \( t_s \). Therefore, the time observed on the Earth's clock is:
\[
t_e = \sqrt{2} \times 3 \text{ years} \approx 4.29 \text{ years}
\]
The time difference between the two clocks on the spaceship is negligible because they are in the same frame of reference. Therefore, the time shown on the clock in the cargo bag when it is compared to a clock on Earth is the same as the time shown on the cockpit clock, which is 3 years.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

